## Nginx Web Root

The custom web root is:

```text
/var/www/devops-app
```

The deployed page is:

```text
/var/www/devops-app/index.html
```

---

## Nginx Site Configuration

The Nginx configuration is generated from:

```text
playbooks/templates/nginx-site.conf.j2
```

The generated configuration is deployed to:

```text
/etc/nginx/sites-available/devops-lab
```

The site is enabled through a symbolic link:

```text
/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/devops-lab
```

The default Nginx site is disabled.

---

## Jinja2 Templates

Two Jinja2 templates are currently used:

```text
playbooks/templates/index.html.j2
playbooks/templates/nginx-site.conf.j2
```

Jinja2 allows Ansible variables and Facts to dynamically generate configuration files.

Example:

```nginx
listen {{ nginx_port }};
```

With:

```yaml
nginx_port: 80
```

the rendered configuration becomes:

```nginx
listen 80;
```

---

## Host-Specific Web Pages

The same HTML template is used for both servers.

Example:

```html
<p>Server: {{ inventory_hostname }}</p>
<p>System Hostname: {{ ansible_hostname }}</p>
<p>Environment: {{ lab_environment }}</p>
```

Ansible generates different output for each Managed Node.

For `web01`:

```text
Server: web01
```

For `web02`:

```text
Server: web02
```

This demonstrates how one template can generate host-specific configuration or content.

---

## Handlers

Handlers are used to reload Nginx only when configuration changes require it.

Example flow:

```text
Nginx Template Changed
        |
        v
      notify
        |
        v
Validate Configuration
        |
        v
     nginx -t
        |
        v
   Reload Nginx
```

If the configuration does not change:

```text
Template = ok
     |
     v
No Handler Execution
```

This avoids unnecessary service reloads.

---

## Nginx Configuration Validation

Before Nginx is reloaded, the configuration is validated using:

```bash
nginx -t
```

The corresponding handler uses:

```yaml
changed_when: false
```

because configuration validation does not modify system state.

This prevents validation commands from being incorrectly reported as infrastructure changes.

---

## Reload vs Restart

Configuration updates use an Nginx reload rather than a full restart.

```text
Restart
   |
   +--> Stop process
   |
   +--> Start process
```

A reload instead allows Nginx to read the new configuration more gracefully.

```text
Reload
   |
   +--> Read new configuration
   |
   +--> Apply configuration
```

---

## HTTP Verification

The playbook performs an HTTP request against each Managed Node after configuration.

The Ansible `uri` module is used:

```yaml
ansible.builtin.uri:
```

The verification expects:

```text
HTTP 200 OK
```

The result is registered into:

```yaml
register: nginx_check
```

and displayed using the `debug` module.

Example:

```text
web01 returned HTTP 200
web02 returned HTTP 200
```

---

## Check Mode Handling

HTTP verification is skipped during Check Mode using:

```yaml
when: not ansible_check_mode
```

This prevents validation steps from expecting resources that have not actually been created during a dry run.

